1
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:31,280
CONVERSATION 1
So, I've been thinking, this summer, instead of our usual holiday rental, I'd like to try renting a camper van and exploring a bit more.

2
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:33,160
A camper van?

3
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:36,080
You mean to spend the summer living out of a car?

4
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:37,740
Yes, a camper van.

5
00:02:37,740 --> 00:02:40,820
And no, it's nothing like living out of a car.

6
00:02:40,820 --> 00:02:45,040
These are proper vans with full-size beds to sleep in.

7
00:02:45,040 --> 00:02:47,120
Some even have kitchens.

8
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:51,560
This way, we wouldn't have to be locked into one place during our holiday.

9
00:02:51,560 --> 00:02:55,760
We'd be able to visit so many more places and travel at our own pace.

10
00:02:55,760 --> 00:03:01,740
The rates of most hotels and holiday homes really soar in the summer anyway.

11
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:04,920
We'd probably save a lot of money as well.

12
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:07,600
I think my grandfather had a van like that.

13
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:10,020
It doesn't sound that appealing to me.

14
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:12,980
Today's camper vans are completely different.

15
00:03:12,980 --> 00:03:16,680
These days, they're equipped with all the modern comforts.

16
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:21,700
I've seen quite a few with solar panels that provide electricity for when you're stopped.

17
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:24,940
This way, you can still use your computer or charge your phone.

18
00:03:24,940 --> 00:03:31,020
And most even have Wi-Fi, so if the weather's not great, you could stream videos.

19
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:33,300
I'm warming up to the idea.

20
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:34,720
Keep talking.

21
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:38,000
Loads of people are committing to this trend full time.

22
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:39,260
Check it out online.

23
00:03:39,260 --> 00:03:45,560
Lots of people stream videos of their alternative lifestyles and actually earn a salary.

24
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:52,220
Imagine having that kind of freedom to go anywhere you want and work from anywhere in the world.

25
00:03:52,220 --> 00:03:53,820
Okay, we can consider it.

26
00:03:53,820 --> 00:03:59,120
As long as the basic needs like toilet and showers are covered, I'm definitely in.

27
00:03:59,120 --> 00:04:02,120
Do you think there will be enough room for my mountain bike?

28
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:03,860
What about food delivery?

29
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:06,200
Will we be able to order to the van?

30
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:08,480
Will there be enough space for the dog?

31
00:04:08,480 --> 00:04:10,320
We wouldn't want to leave him behind.

32
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:11,460
Slow down.

33
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:13,060
Glad you're excited about it.

34
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:23,100
Let's start by looking at what's available first.

35
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:40,560
Q1. What has the woman been thinking of doing for this summer?

36
00:04:40,560 --> 00:04:59,080
Q2. What do we learn about the man's grandfather from the conversation?

37
00:04:59,080 --> 00:05:17,520
Q3. What can many camper vans do nowadays according to the woman?

38
00:05:17,520 --> 00:05:35,380
Q4. What idea does the woman find appealing about the camper van lifestyle?

39
00:05:35,380 --> 00:05:37,140
CONVERSATION 2
You wanted to see me?

40
00:05:37,140 --> 00:05:39,560
Yes, I'll get straight to the point.

41
00:05:39,560 --> 00:05:43,440
Over the past month or so, it has become apparent that your performance has dipped.

42
00:05:43,440 --> 00:05:49,740
You've been late to work several times these past few weeks, which is very uncharacteristic of you.

43
00:05:49,740 --> 00:05:53,500
But what really concerns me is your curtailed output.

44
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:56,660
Lately, you seem to be constantly one step behind.

45
00:05:56,660 --> 00:05:57,840
What's going on?

46
00:05:57,840 --> 00:05:58,720
I'm sorry.

47
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:02,480
I'm going through some unfortunate family issues at the moment.

48
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:07,380
It's a personal matter, so if you don't mind, I much prefer we don't get into it.

49
00:06:07,380 --> 00:06:11,660
Nevertheless, I expect it to have all blown over in another month or so.

50
00:06:11,660 --> 00:06:14,620
Do you need some time off?

51
00:06:14,620 --> 00:06:15,620
Actually, yes.

52
00:06:15,620 --> 00:06:18,620
Would it be possible to take the next two Mondays off?

53
00:06:18,620 --> 00:06:22,960
I was deliberating whether I should ask for this, but now that we're here...

54
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:24,040
Sure thing.

55
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:27,680
But if you needed some leave, why didn't you ask for it earlier?

56
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:29,140
I'm not sure why.

57
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:32,200
I guess I kind of felt bad asking for it.

58
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:36,000
You shouldn't feel bad asking for leave, especially if you need it.

59
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:36,720
I know.

60
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:37,380
You're right.

61
00:06:37,380 --> 00:06:37,900
I'm sorry.

62
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:44,700
Look, you've been with us almost five years and I personally really value your proficiency at your job.

63
00:06:44,700 --> 00:06:46,860
It makes my job far easier.

64
00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:52,160
In addition, I can assure you that management as a whole also holds you in high regard.

65
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:53,260
Thank you.

66
00:06:53,260 --> 00:06:56,420
What I need is for you to be back at your 100%.

67
00:06:56,420 --> 00:06:59,920
Are you sure taking two Mondays off will suffice?

68
00:06:59,920 --> 00:07:00,820
It'll be enough.

69
00:07:00,820 --> 00:07:02,000
I'm sure of it.

70
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:03,200
Okay, good.

71
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:04,780
So this is what we're going to do.

72
00:07:04,780 --> 00:07:10,000
You will take those days off, and I expect that you will gradually come back to your usual self.

73
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:14,420
Then in two months' time, we will catch up again to see how you are faring.

74
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:15,300
Sounds good.

75
00:07:15,300 --> 00:07:24,080
Thank you for your understanding.

76
00:07:24,080 --> 00:07:41,660
Q5. What is the man's main concern about the woman?

77
00:07:41,660 --> 00:07:59,500
Q6. What does the woman say is causing her problem?

78
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:17,400
Q7. What does the man value about the woman?

79
00:08:17,400 --> 00:09:04,420
Q8. What agreement have the speakers reached by the end of the conversation?

80
00:09:04,420 --> 00:09:08,660
PASSAGE 1
There are many factors that impact our health.

81
00:09:08,660 --> 00:09:13,600
These include our finances, our emotions and the way we work.

82
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:15,420
Education is one such factor.

83
00:09:15,420 --> 00:09:23,600
Research suggests that higher levels of education can lead to better health and even greater longevity.

84
00:09:23,600 --> 00:09:26,480
But what about the education of your partner?

85
00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:29,780
New research seeks to answer this question.

86
00:09:29,780 --> 00:09:33,700
Researchers use data from a longitudinal study.

87
00:09:33,700 --> 00:09:39,740
The data came from surveys first administered to high school seniors in 1957.

88
00:09:39,740 --> 00:09:41,940
The surveys ended in 2011.

89
00:09:41,940 --> 00:09:50,200
The team found that a person whose spouse had a higher level of education tended to report better health.

90
00:09:50,200 --> 00:10:00,980
This remained the case when adjusting for other factors, such as participants' own education, social background and health status before and after marriage.

91
00:10:00,980 --> 00:10:09,360
The authors suggest that high levels of education provide shared social, intellectual, emotional and financial resources.

92
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:18,820
These include things like higher levels of emotional intelligence, particular skills or knowledge, or more money.

93
00:10:18,820 --> 00:10:32,360
The researchers concede that an individual's own level of education is clearly important for their health, but they assert that individuals also can reap the benefits of their partner's education.

94
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:35,020
It is noteworthy that the effects vary by gender.

95
00:10:35,020 --> 00:10:42,040
For women, health was more closely correlated to their spouse's education than for men.

96
00:10:42,040 --> 00:10:46,880
The team suggests this may be related to the time period of the study.

97
00:10:46,880 --> 00:10:53,460
Women in this sample had lower levels of education and lower occupational achievements than the men.

98
00:10:53,460 --> 00:11:08,200
The researchers' hypothesis is that this made the husband's resources more likely to have a shared impact.

99
00:11:08,200 --> 00:11:26,460
Q9. What does research suggest about a higher level of education?

100
00:11:26,460 --> 00:11:44,300
Q10. What do we learn from the finding of the new research?

101
00:11:44,300 --> 00:12:02,860
Q11. What does the passage say about the men in the sample of the new research?

102
00:12:02,860 --> 00:12:09,160
PASSAGE 2
Climate scientists predict that many parts of the world will be increasingly prone to floods.

103
00:12:09,160 --> 00:12:12,760
This is a serious risk to human lives and property.

104
00:12:12,760 --> 00:12:20,000
So understanding and accurately forecasting flood risks is a key challenge for vulnerable cities around the globe.

105
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:27,560
In 2017, Derek Loftis of the Virginia Institute of Marine Science started a project called Catch the King.

106
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:34,040
It uses a smartphone app to collect the data of ordinary citizens during high tides.

107
00:12:34,040 --> 00:12:40,760
He'll use those data to validate and improve his mathematical flooding model, called TideWatch.

108
00:12:40,760 --> 00:12:44,560
Loftis's mission is to know where the water goes before it goes there.

109
00:12:44,560 --> 00:12:53,200
But collecting necessary data and then processing that information quickly enough to make usable forecasts is anything but easy.

110
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:57,460
Accurate flood forecasts require an understanding of the situation on the ground.

111
00:12:57,460 --> 00:13:07,320
By the early 2000s, supercomputing had advanced enough that scientists could integrate weather forecasting models with such measurements.

112
00:13:07,320 --> 00:13:14,020
But they still didn't have the critically important ability to forecast fast-moving floods in real time.

113
00:13:14,020 --> 00:13:25,340
So, in 2017, Loftis and colleagues set up a group of 28 Internet-connected water-level sensors throughout the flood risk community of Norfolk, Virginia.

114
00:13:25,340 --> 00:13:33,680
The new sensors relayed rough measurements about water height and movement to a computer at the Virginia Institute.

115
00:13:33,680 --> 00:13:41,960
Along with those sensors, hundreds of local citizens take photos of rising tides and transmit them in real time to the institute.

116
00:13:41,960 --> 00:13:58,160
Scientists hope that the data collected by these citizens can help sharpen the ability to forecast exactly when and where damaging floods will occur.

117
00:13:58,160 --> 00:14:20,560
Q12. What is a key challenge for vulnerable cities around the globe according to the passage?

118
00:14:20,560 --> 00:14:38,293
Q13. Why did Derek Loftis start the "Catch the King" project?

119
00:14:38,293 --> 00:14:56,960
Q14. What was the problem with flood forecasting by the early 2000s?

120
00:14:56,960 --> 00:15:42,900
Q15. What did Loftis and his colleagues do in 2017 to improve flood forecasting?

121
00:15:42,900 --> 00:16:00,700
RECORDING 1
Now, it's become something of a ritual to debate the value of a college degree, but as Current Population Survey data show, there has been a sharp decline in employment among men without college degrees compared with those who have them.

122
00:16:00,700 --> 00:16:07,020
This decline began in the 1960s and has become more serious over time.

123
00:16:07,020 --> 00:16:09,980
What has actually caused this decline?

124
00:16:09,980 --> 00:16:17,940
Well, economists have been working to ascertain the roots of the decline for decades and have come up with several theories.

125
00:16:17,940 --> 00:16:23,780
One popular theory is that it's a case of too low wages for jobs that don't require a degree.

126
00:16:23,780 --> 00:16:31,560
An alternate theory is that there were more jobs not requiring a college degree in the past than now.

127
00:16:31,560 --> 00:16:41,780
A recent working paper from the National Bureau of Economic Research reviewed those theories and put forward additional hypotheses about the causes of the problem.

128
00:16:41,780 --> 00:16:44,420
Let's look at that paper now.

129
00:16:44,420 --> 00:16:48,160
First, the researchers considered the issue of wages.

130
00:16:48,160 --> 00:17:00,080
As the wage gap has grown between those with college degrees and those without, economists have suggested that men aren't as interested in taking some of the less lucrative jobs.

131
00:17:00,080 --> 00:17:20,340
While the authors of the paper found truth in this explanation, they assert that low wages can't be the only factor, as wages for men without college degrees, adjusting for inflation, haven't changed much since the 1960s, making it hard to see why fewer men seem willing to accept those wages.

132
00:17:20,340 --> 00:17:26,960
This leads to the second theory, that more jobs require a college degree today than in the past.

133
00:17:26,960 --> 00:17:36,700
Indeed, that theory seems valid, because in America today, 2/3 of jobs are filled by workers with at least some college education.

134
00:17:36,700 --> 00:17:42,060
However, the authors of the paper argue that there are other factors amplifying the problem.

135
00:17:42,060 --> 00:18:01,900
They identified two new potential explanations: the first being the tremendous decline in marriage among men without college degrees since the 1960s; and the second, being the increase in women participating in the workforce that was concurrent with the decline in marriage.

136
00:18:01,900 --> 00:18:15,180
These two changes mean that men might not have families to support, or if they do, their wives might be doing more of the providing, both of which might remove the financial incentive to work.

137
00:18:15,180 --> 00:18:35,132
The authors of the paper concede that research on the subject isn't complete, but they argue that their work supports the value of a college education.

138
00:18:35,132 --> 00:18:50,480
Q16. What does the speaker say has become something of a ritual?

139
00:18:50,480 --> 00:19:09,680
Q17. What did the researchers of a recent working paper consider first?

140
00:19:09,680 --> 00:19:31,780
Q18. What did the recent paper identify as a new potential explanation of the problem concerning men's employment?

141
00:19:31,780 --> 00:19:44,940
RECORDING 2
While an increasing number of people are trying to eat less meat, a market research team has found in its latest fashion and sustainability report that the trend is now spreading from kitchen to closet.

142
00:19:44,940 --> 00:19:54,560
It found animal welfare came top of a list of issues that people considered before buying clothes, with 42% saying it was important to them.

143
00:19:54,560 --> 00:20:11,240
The researchers of the team predicted that the next year would see a boom in animal-free shoe collections, since they heard shoppers of all ages say they would buy footwear designated as "vegan", meaning the goods were produced without using any animal or animal part.

144
00:20:11,240 --> 00:20:20,760
As well as being on trend, and with a much better ring to it than synthetic leather, the vegan label does convey an important extra distinction.

145
00:20:20,760 --> 00:20:26,300
If you label something as vegan, then the whole product needs to be animal free.

146
00:20:26,300 --> 00:20:33,640
That means checking things like the glue that holds the shoe together, for example, and the chemicals used for finishing them.

147
00:20:33,640 --> 00:20:39,640
At the top end, some of the world-renowned fashion designers have avoided leather and fur for some time.

148
00:20:39,640 --> 00:20:54,900
In fact, The Animal Free Product Society says they've seen a boom in products registered with the vegan trademark.

149
00:20:54,900 --> 00:20:57,960
In 2018, there were 119.

150
00:20:57,960 --> 00:21:30,600
So far this year, one of the first reputable store chains to use the vegan trademark, prices ballet shoes at £7.

151
00:21:30,600 --> 00:21:34,940
99 and a vegan laptop handbag at £29.

152
00:21:34,940 --> 00:22:13,700
99, comparable with its other products.

153
00:22:13,700 --> 00:22:44,200
Q19. What trend does the market research team find is spreading from kitchen to closet?

154
00:22:44,200 --> 00:23:03,640
Q20. What have some world-renowned fashion designers done for some time?

155
00:23:03,640 --> 00:23:23,000
Q21. What does the vegan label mean for a product?

156
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:36,900
RECORDING 3
By the end of 2016, anyone who had been paying even passing attention to the news headlines was highly likely to conclude that everything was terrible, and that the world was going to hell.

157
00:23:36,900 --> 00:23:44,660
It was easy to feel depressed by the wars in different parts of the world, the spread of deadly viruses or terror attacks.

158
00:23:44,660 --> 00:23:50,260
Yet, 2016 was the best year in the history of humanity.

159
00:23:50,260 --> 00:23:55,240
Child mortality was roughly half what it was as recently as 1990.

160
00:23:55,240 --> 00:24:00,460
And 300,000 more people gained access to electricity each day.

161
00:24:00,460 --> 00:24:12,720
Every single measure of material and environmental welfare in America, Europe, Asia, Africa and the world as a whole has improved rather than deteriorated.

162
00:24:12,720 --> 00:24:19,680
We are living in history's most peaceful era, with violence of all kinds in steep decline.

163
00:24:19,680 --> 00:24:21,180
Those are the facts.

164
00:24:21,180 --> 00:24:23,900
So why aren't we all optimists?

165
00:24:23,900 --> 00:24:35,320
In a 2015 survey for YouGov, 65% of British people and 81% of the French said they thought the world was getting worse.

166
00:24:35,320 --> 00:24:41,340
However, according to numerous sensible statistics, these people are simply wrong.

167
00:24:41,340 --> 00:24:45,400
Nobody in their right mind should wish to have lived in a previous century.

168
00:24:45,400 --> 00:24:47,760
Yet people do.

169
00:24:47,760 --> 00:24:49,460
Why?

170
00:24:49,460 --> 00:24:52,780
Our prevailing mood of despair is irrational.

171
00:24:52,780 --> 00:24:57,000
It says more about us than it does about how things really are.

172
00:24:57,000 --> 00:25:11,080
It is best explained as the result of various psychological biases that served a purpose for our evolutionary ancestors— but now, in a media-saturated era, constantly mislead us.

173
00:25:11,080 --> 00:25:53,673
Once upon a time, in our evolutionary past, it was a very good thing that our attention could be easily seized by negative information, as it alerted us to potential dangers.

174
00:25:53,673 --> 00:26:22,526
But in today's world, this bias means that we are constantly bombarded with bad news, which distorts our perception of reality.

175
00:26:22,526 --> 00:26:47,257
Q22. What does the speaker say about the year 2016?

176
00:26:47,257 --> 00:27:09,240
Q23. What did the 2015 YouGov survey find?

177
00:27:09,240 --> 00:27:09,290
Q24. What does the speaker say explains our prevailing mood of despair?

178
00:27:09,290 --> 00:27:53,638
Q25. Why was it beneficial for our evolutionary ancestors to be easily attracted by negative information?
